{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"da84af66feb049c5","type":"text","text":"\n## **11:35** -\tWhat are *Questions and Negations*\n## **11:55** - Do ex. *51/52/53/54/55*\n\n## **12:15** - Wrap-up and homework reminder\n\n","x":-125,"y":-20,"width":811,"height":147,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"573a53e07c08edfe","type":"text","text":"# **Today's Class: Questions and Negations**","x":-19,"y":-107,"width":600,"height":60,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"9dd3765de3dc014c","type":"text","text":"## Required Knowledge \n**Subject**: the person or thing that does the action.  \n    Example: **She** plays football.\n    \n**Verb**: the action or state in the sentence.  \n    Example: She **plays** football.\n    \n**Word order**: the position of words changes in questions and negatives.  \n    Statement: _You like pizza._  \n    Question: _Do you like pizza?_  \n    Negative: _You do not like pizza._","x":893,"y":-107,"width":790,"height":280,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"afa2324187661bc0","type":"text","text":"## **Study Grammar 3 (p. 79).**","x":-125,"y":158,"width":356,"height":92,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"88786c6b3610aa49","type":"text","text":"## The **new grammar rule** is about how to turn normal sentences into **questions** and **negations**.","x":1753,"y":173,"width":640,"height":187,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"74da40d63458416c","type":"text","text":"\n\n## If the sentence has _to be_ or an auxiliary verb\n\nExamples of these verbs are **am, is, are, was, were, can, have, has, will**.\n\n### Making a question\n\nMove the verb to the beginning.\n\n**Statement:**  \nHe **is** doing his homework.\n\n**Question:**  \n**Is** he doing his homework?\n\nAnother example:\n\n**Statement:**  \nYou **can** read that sign.\n\n**Question:**  \n**Can** you read that sign?\n\nSo:\n\n**verb + subject + rest of sentence?**","x":1753,"y":445,"width":640,"height":602,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"ac6e38bac8954eeb","type":"text","text":"### Making a negation\n\nKeep the verb where it is and add **not** after it.\n\n**Statement:**  \nThey **were** watching TV.\n\n**Negative:**  \nThey **were not** watching TV.  \nThey **weren't** watching TV.\n\nOther examples:\n\n- I **have not heard** from her yet.\n- He **wasn't** very pleased.\n- We **won't** come here again.","x":2575,"y":445,"width":723,"height":558,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"a9b08db7b65c49a2","type":"text","text":"## If there is only a normal main verb\n\nExamples: **play, sleep, like, tell, read, work**\n\nThen you need to add **do, does or did**.\n\n### Present simple questions\n\nUse:\n\n**do/does + subject + infinitive**\n\n**Statement:**  \nYou like football.\n\n**Question:**  \n**Do** you **like** football?\n\nWith **he/she/it**, use **does**:\n\n**Statement:**  \nShe likes football.\n\n**Question:**  \n**Does** she **like** football?\n\nNotice:\n\nNot: _Does she likes football?_  \nCorrect: **Does she like football?**\n\nThe **-s disappears** because _does_ already shows the tense.","x":1809,"y":1480,"width":529,"height":720,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"ea3bd98eb4a2c44b","type":"text","text":"## Present simple negations\n\nUse:\n\n**subject + do/does + not + infinitive**\n\n**I play football.**  \n→ I **don't play** football.\n\n**The cat sleeps in our bed.**  \n→ The cat **doesn't sleep** in our bed.\n\nAgain:\n\nNot: _The cat doesn't sleeps._  \nCorrect: **The cat doesn't sleep.**","x":2575,"y":1480,"width":544,"height":499,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"01e53e27a7416e88","type":"text","text":"## Past simple questions\n\nUse:\n\n**did + subject + infinitive**\n\n**Statement:**  \nHe told you about the trip.\n\n**Question:**  \n**Did** he **tell** you about the trip?\n\nThis is very important:\n\nNot: _Did he told you?_  \nCorrect: **Did he tell you?**\n\nAfter **did**, the verb goes back to its basic form.\n\n**went → did go**  \n**told → did tell**  \n**saw → did see**","x":3796,"y":445,"width":543,"height":667,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"c63e6e10a166773d","type":"text","text":"## Past simple negations\n\nUse:\n\n**subject + did not + infinitive**\n\n**She went to school.**  \n→ She **didn't go** to school.\n\n**He told me.**  \n→ He **didn't tell** me.\n\nNot: _He didn't told me._","x":4429,"y":445,"width":479,"height":466,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"38cf67760fbcc00c","type":"text","text":"### Questions & Negations : Quick Overview\n\n**1. Already have _to be_ or an auxiliary verb?**  \nUse that verb.\n\n- She **is** working. → **Is** she working?\n- She **is** working. → She **isn't** working.\n- He **can** swim. → **Can** he swim?\n\n**2. No auxiliary verb?**  \nUse **do / does / did**.\n\n- You play. → **Do** you play?\n- She plays. → **Does** she play?\n- He played. → **Did** he play?\n\n**3. Negatives**\n\n- I **don't** play.\n- She **doesn't** play.\n- He **didn't** play.\n\n**4. Important:**  \nAfter **do / does / did**, always use the **infinitive**.\n\nCorrect: **Did he go?**  \nWrong: _Did he went?_","x":-125,"y":445,"width":492,"height":758,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"8b5849a0ffb10dec","x":893,"y":218,"width":790,"height":202,"color":"5","type":"text","text":"## Tense\n\n**Present and past verb forms**: verbs change depending on time.  \n    Example: _play_ becomes _played_, _go_ becomes _went_.\n    \n**Auxiliary verb**: a helping verb used to make questions and negative sentences.  \n    Examples: **do, does, did, is, are, was, were, have, has, will**."},
		{"id":"3723602205aa2090","x":403,"y":445,"width":352,"height":839,"color":"6","type":"text","text":"### Tenses: Quick Overview\n\n**Present simple**  \nHabit / fact\n\n- I **play** football.\n- She **plays** football.\n\n**Present continuous**  \nHappening now\n\n- I **am playing**.\n- She **is playing**.\n- They **are playing**.\n\n**Past simple**  \nFinished action in the past\n\n- I **played** football.\n- She **went** home.\n\n**Past continuous**  \nAction in progress in the past\n\n- I **was playing**.\n- They **were playing**.\n\n### Quick rule\n\n- **Present simple** = usually / always\n- **Present continuous** = happening now\n- **Past simple** = finished past action\n- **Past continuous** = was happening"}
	],
